  is as  a human being you use a lot of services  you use your smartphone you use the  internet  you use a car so those are things that  you can  definitely choose to buy stuff  appropriately  and the other thing is as a software  developer you work on  software and it's in when i try to  research more into it i realize that  four percent of the total carbon  footprint on this planet  is contributed by software so i know  that it is not  four percent seems very minuscule  compared to you know 20  30 that we have out there with cars with  with you know agriculture and other  things  so but if you can make a dent in that  four percent  because that is completely in your  control that would really go a long way  in trying to make this planet better  so that was what was the motivation that  i had and then i connected it back to  what i had learned in school  so i'm from carnegie mellon university  in school we had  lectures around you know how can you  write more efficient software  and then i try to club these two  thoughts together can we  write software that is efficient and  better for the environment and there are  simple techniques as software engineers  that you can employ in your day-to-day  life so that's where you know these dots  connected and that's how i got  interested  into it and i've been using it in my  work since that point on and i am a  strong advocate of you know having  making a change in the environment  because we should always leave back  the world better than what we got it so  that's the motivation  so bitcoin i i'm not surprised to hear  that that that was an inspiration there  when people think about this topic  that's you know the first thing they  think of because  you know there's a lot of talk about it  so based on what you've learned  what do you think the hope is for  bitcoin for what could be done  there to reduce the carbon footprint  sure so one thing is that there is also  a misnomer out there  that bitcoin energy is like bitcoin is  really bad for the environment  it is bad because it uses a lot of  energy but if you compare it to the  industries it is trying to disrupt such  as traditional banking or  gold the energy usage in those areas or  those industries is still much higher  than bitcoin  and if you look at what the aim of  bitcoin is it is  to actually give financial independence  to people that are not  really reached out by traditional  banking so it really will make people's  lives better  so in that sense i still feel that you  know the energy usage in bitcoin is a  problem  but there are far bigger problems as  well out there for ener for reducing the  energy consumption that's  the first point uh the second point is  that yes proof of work is bad for the  environment  so it is unfortunately the way bitcoin  is designed  it will keep using proof of work there  are alternators such as ethereum  which use proof of stake and with  ethereum 2.0  the amount of energy will go down by  99.9 percent  when s 2.0 just comes out so  so that's the other thing i have seen  that recently bitcoin  proof of work algorithms like the mining  farms and all they are using renewable  energy sources  at locations where previously we were  not able to tap into them  so a very good example is el salvador  which is introducing bitcoin they  actually want to mine  it using the volcano that they have so  energy from the volcano  so i think these are interesting ideas  that people are coming up with  to circumvent the problems and these  ideas can also be applied in other  industries  so it's not really a lose-lose i still  feel that there is  something to gain from our experience  with bitcoin and with  increase in usability increase in  scalability things will get better  that's what i think yeah it's nice to  talk to somebody who  has some real perspective on on it and  has thought through the problems  as opposed to just bitcoin is bad for  the environment  what do we do so you work at netflix and  it